<body>
  <Header />
  <main>
  <article class="container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <span class="tag">Productivity</span>
      <h1>Notion vs Confluence: Which Documentation Tool Is Better in 2026?</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated July 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lead">
      Notion and Confluence are the two documentation tools most often shortlisted by teams in 2026, and they take opposite approaches. Notion wins on flexibility, flat-rate pricing, and ease of use for small to mid-size teams. Confluence wins on enterprise governance, Jira integration, and compliance certifications. This comparison breaks down features, permissions, integrations, pricing, and use cases so you can pick the right one for your team.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-top">
      <a href="https://www.notion.com" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Notion Free</a>
      <a href="https://www.atlassian.com/software/confluence" class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Confluence Free</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Quick Comparison</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Notion (Plus)</th><th>Confluence (Standard)</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Best For</td><td>Small to mid-size teams, all-in-one workspace</td><td>Enterprise, engineering, Atlassian ecosystem</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Price</td><td>$10/mo flat for workspace</td><td>$5.75/user/mo</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Free Tier</td><td>Unlimited pages for individuals</td><td>Up to 3 users, 2 GB storage</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pricing Model</td><td>Flat rate per workspace</td><td>Per user per month</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Editor Type</td><td>Block-based, flexible</td><td>Rich text, structured templates</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Databases</td><td>Native, multiple views</td><td>Limited, via page properties</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Jira Integration</td><td>Third-party only</td><td>Native, bidirectional</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Page Permissions</td><td>Page-level, basic</td><td>Granular, space and page level</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Audit Logs</td><td>Enterprise plan only</td><td>Standard plan and above</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Approval Workflows</td><td>Manual, via databases</td><td>Native page approvals</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AI Features</td><td>Notion AI, writing and Q&A</td><td>Atlassian Intelligence, summarization</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Compliance</td><td>SOC 2, GDPR on Enterprise</td><td>SOC 2, FedRAMP, ISO 27001</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mobile Apps</td><td>iOS, Android</td><td>iOS, Android</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Offline Access</td><td>Limited, mobile only</td><td>No native offline</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What Is Notion?</h2>
    <p>Notion is an all-in-one workspace built by Notion Labs, founded in 2013 by Ivan Zhao and Simon Last and based in San Francisco. Notion launched in 2016 with a mission to combine docs, wikis, databases, and project management into a single flexible tool. By 2026, Notion is the leading choice for small to mid-size teams that want one workspace for product specs, meeting notes, knowledge bases, and lightweight project tracking without paying for separate tools.</p>
    <p>Notion's defining feature is its block-based editor. Every paragraph, heading, image, or database is a block that can be dragged, nested, and rearranged. Databases in Notion are first-class citizens, supporting table, board, calendar, gallery, and timeline views on the same data. This flexibility lets a single Notion workspace serve as a wiki, a project tracker, a content calendar, and a CRM at the same time. Pricing is flat-rate: the Plus plan is $10 per month for the entire workspace, Business is $18 per user per month, and Enterprise is custom.</p>

    <h2>What Is Confluence?</h2>
    <p>Confluence is a team documentation and wiki platform built by Atlassian, founded in 2002 and based in Sydney, Australia. Confluence launched in 2004 as a wiki alternative to Microsoft SharePoint and has since become the enterprise standard for technical documentation, engineering knowledge bases, and project collaboration. By 2026, Confluence is the default choice for organizations already using Jira, Bitbucket, or other Atlassian products.</p>
    <p>Confluence's defining feature is its structured approach to documentation. Pages are organized into spaces with hierarchical page trees, and each space has its own permission set. Confluence offers granular page-level permissions, native approval workflows, audit logs, and compliance certifications including SOC 2, FedRAMP, and ISO 27001. The editor is rich text with template support for RFCs, architecture decisions, meeting notes, and retrospectives. Pricing is per user per month: Free for up to 3 users, Standard at $5.75 per user, Premium at $11 per user, and Enterprise at custom pricing.</p>

    <h2>Editor and Content Structure</h2>
    <p>The editor is where you spend most of your time, and the two tools take fundamentally different approaches. Notion is block-based and flexible. Confluence is template-driven and structured.</p>

    <h3>Notion Block Editor</h3>
    <p>Notion treats every piece of content as a block. Paragraphs, headings, images, code snippets, callouts, and databases are all blocks that can be dragged, nested, and converted into one another. This makes Notion extremely flexible for teams that want to build custom workflows. You can create a product spec with inline databases for tracking tasks, embed a Figma file, and add a meeting notes toggle, all on a single page. The trade-off is that Notion pages can become cluttered if teams do not establish conventions.</p>

    <h3>Confluence Template Editor</h3>
    <p>Confluence uses a rich text editor with strong template support. Pages are typically created from templates like "Meeting Notes", "Decision Record", or "How-To Article", which enforces structure from the start. Confluence macros, such as the Jira Issues macro, Table of Contents, and Status macro, add dynamic functionality inside pages. The structured approach is better for enterprise governance, where consistency and auditability matter more than flexibility.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best flexibility goes to <span class="winner-tag">Notion</span>. Best structured consistency goes to <span class="winner-tag">Confluence</span>.</p>

    <h2>Permissions and Governance</h2>
    <p>Permissions and governance are where Confluence has a clear lead for enterprise use. Notion is simpler but less granular.</p>

    <h3>Notion Permissions</h3>
    <p>Notion offers page-level permissions on Business and Enterprise plans. You can share a page with specific people, restrict editing to certain roles, and control whether a page is public within the workspace. However, Notion does not offer space-level permission sets like Confluence, and audit logs are only available on the Enterprise plan. For small teams, this is fine. For regulated industries, it can be a blocker.</p>

    <h3>Confluence Permissions</h3>
    <p>Confluence has the most granular permission system in the documentation category. Permissions exist at the space level, page level, and even individual comment level. Global permissions control who can create spaces, export content, or administer the instance. Audit logs are available on Standard and above, and Confluence supports approval workflows where a page cannot be published until a designated reviewer signs off. This level of control is why regulated industries default to Confluence.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best permissions and governance goes to <span class="winner-tag">Confluence</span>.</p>

    <h2>Jira Integration and Atlassian Ecosystem</h2>
    <p>Integration with engineering workflows is a defining difference. Confluence is built by Atlassian, the same company that builds Jira, and the integration is deep and bidirectional. Notion has no native Jira integration.</p>

    <h3>Confluence and Jira</h3>
    <p>Confluence and Jira share a single Atlassian account, so authentication is seamless. Inside a Confluence page, you can embed a Jira Issues macro that shows live tickets, filters issues by status or assignee, and updates when the Jira ticket changes. Jira tickets can link back to Confluence pages for specs and architecture decisions. This bidirectional linking is critical for engineering teams that want requirements, tickets, and documentation to stay in sync automatically.</p>

    <h3>Notion and Third-Party Integrations</h3>
    <p>Notion integrates with Jira through third-party tools like Zapier, Make, or unofficial embeds. These integrations can display Jira tickets inside a Notion page, but they do not offer the bidirectional linking, live updates, or deep filtering that Confluence provides natively. If Jira is central to your engineering workflow, Notion will feel like a workaround rather than an integration.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best Jira integration goes to <span class="winner-tag">Confluence</span>.</p>

    <h2>Databases and Project Management</h2>
    <p>Databases are where Notion has a decisive advantage. Notion databases are first-class objects with multiple views, while Confluence treats structured data as a secondary feature.</p>

    <h3>Notion Databases</h3>
    <p>Notion databases support table, board, calendar, gallery, timeline, and list views on the same underlying data. You can add properties like assignee, due date, status, priority, and tags, then filter, sort, and group by any property. A single Notion database can serve as a task tracker, a content calendar, a CRM, and a knowledge base index, all depending on the view. This makes Notion a genuine all-in-one workspace for teams that want to replace Trello, Airtable, and a separate wiki with one tool.</p>

    <h3>Confluence Page Properties</h3>
    <p>Confluence has page properties that can be displayed in a Page Properties Report macro, which creates a simple table of pages with their metadata. This is useful for generating lists of decisions, meeting notes, or technical specs. However, Confluence databases are not as flexible as Notion's, and building a kanban board or calendar view requires additional Atlassian tools like Jira or Team Calendars. For lightweight project management, Notion is significantly stronger.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best databases and project management goes to <span class="winner-tag">Notion</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pricing Comparison</h2>
    <p>Pricing is one of the biggest differentiators. Notion uses flat-rate pricing per workspace, while Confluence charges per user per month. The right plan depends on your team size and growth trajectory.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Notion</th><th>Confluence</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Free</td><td>Unlimited pages for individuals</td><td>Up to 3 users, 2 GB storage</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Basic Paid</td><td>$10/mo flat (Plus)</td><td>$5.75/user/mo (Standard)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mid-tier</td><td>$18/user/mo (Business)</td><td>$11/user/mo (Premium)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Enterprise</td><td>Custom (Enterprise)</td><td>Custom (Enterprise)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cost at 10 users</td><td>$10/mo (Plus)</td><td>$57.50/mo</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cost at 50 users</td><td>$10/mo (Plus)</td><td>$287.50/mo</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cost at 100 users</td><td>$180/mo (Business)</td><td>$575/mo</td></tr>
        <tr><td>AI Add-on</td><td>$10/user/mo (Notion AI)</td><td>Included on Premium and above</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The pricing comparison is stark at scale. Notion Plus at $10 per month for the entire workspace is the most cost-effective option for small to mid-size teams. At 50 users, Notion Plus costs $10 per month while Confluence Standard costs $287.50 per month. Even at 100 users on Notion Business ($18 per user), the cost is $1,800 per month, which is higher than Confluence Standard but includes significantly more flexibility as an all-in-one workspace.</p>
    <p>For enterprise organizations, Confluence's per-user pricing is predictable and integrates with Atlassian's broader billing. For startups and growing teams, Notion's flat-rate pricing is hard to beat.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best value for small teams goes to <span class="winner-tag">Notion</span>. Best enterprise predictability goes to <span class="winner-tag">Confluence</span>.</p>

    <h2>AI Features</h2>
    <p>Both Notion and Confluence have invested heavily in AI, but they focus on different use cases. Notion AI is better for content creation, while Confluence Intelligence is better for engineering context.</p>

    <h3>Notion AI</h3>
    <p>Notion AI offers writing assistance, summarization, Q&A across your workspace, and autofill for database properties. You can ask Notion AI to draft a page from a prompt, summarize a long meeting note, or answer questions like "what is our refund policy?" by searching your workspace. The autofill feature is particularly powerful for databases, where Notion AI can categorize tasks or extract action items automatically. Notion AI costs $10 per user per month as an add-on.</p>

    <h3>Confluence Intelligence</h3>
    <p>Confluence Intelligence, powered by Atlassian Intelligence, focuses on summarizing pages, generating Jira issues from text, and answering questions across Atlassian products. You can ask Confluence to summarize a long thread, draft a reply to a comment, or convert a meeting note into Jira tickets. Confluence Intelligence is included on Premium and Enterprise plans at no extra cost. The strength is engineering context: because Confluence is tied to Jira, the AI can answer questions like "what tickets are blocking this release?" by pulling live data from Jira.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for writers goes to <span class="winner-tag">Notion</span>. Best for engineering context goes to <span class="winner-tag">Confluence</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pros and Cons</h2>

    <h3>Notion Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Flat-rate pricing at $10 per month for the entire workspace</li>
        <li>Flexible block-based editor that adapts to any workflow</li>
        <li>Powerful databases with multiple views on the same data</li>
        <li>All-in-one workspace for docs, wikis, projects, and CRM</li>
        <li>Notion AI excels at writing assistance and workspace Q&A</li>
        <li>Beautiful, minimalist design that is easy for new users</li>
        <li>Strong templates gallery for common team workflows</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Notion Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>No native Jira integration, requires third-party workarounds</li>
        <li>Limited page-level permissions without Enterprise plan</li>
        <li>Audit logs only available on Enterprise plan</li>
        <li>No native approval workflows for compliance-heavy teams</li>
        <li>Can become disorganized without team conventions</li>
        <li>Limited offline access, mobile only</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Confluence Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Native, bidirectional Jira integration with live ticket macros</li>
        <li>Granular permissions at space, page, and comment level</li>
        <li>Audit logs on Standard plan and above</li>
        <li>Native approval workflows for regulated documentation</li>
        <li>Compliance certifications including SOC 2, FedRAMP, ISO 27001</li>
        <li>Structured templates enforce consistency at scale</li>
        <li>Atlassian Intelligence included on Premium plans</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Confluence Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Per-user pricing adds up quickly for growing teams</li>
        <li>Editor is less flexible than Notion's block-based approach</li>
        <li>Databases are limited compared to Notion's multi-view system</li>
        <li>Steeper learning curve for non-technical users</li>
        <li>Best features require Jira or other Atlassian tools</li>
        <li>No native offline mode</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Who Should Use Which?</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Startups and Small Teams (1-50 people)</h3>
      <p>If you are a startup or small team that wants one tool for docs, wikis, and project tracking, Notion is the clear choice. The flat-rate Plus plan at $10 per month covers your entire workspace, and the flexible editor lets you build a custom workflow without paying for separate tools. Notion's databases replace Trello for kanban, Airtable for structured data, and a separate wiki for documentation. For small teams, Notion is the most cost-effective and flexible option.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Engineering Teams Using Jira</h3>
      <p>If your engineering team already uses Jira for issue tracking, Confluence is the natural documentation companion. The native bidirectional integration means your specs, architecture decisions, and tickets stay in sync automatically. Confluence's templates for RFCs and ADRs, combined with code blocks and approval workflows, make it the standard for engineering documentation. Switching to Notion would mean losing the Jira integration that makes Confluence valuable.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Enterprise and Regulated Industries</h3>
      <p>If you work in finance, healthcare, government, or another regulated industry, Confluence is the safer choice. Granular permissions, audit logs, approval workflows, and compliance certifications like FedRAMP and ISO 27001 are table stakes for regulated documentation. Notion's Enterprise plan offers SOC 2 and GDPR compliance, but it lacks the depth of Confluence's governance features. For audits and compliance reviews, Confluence is easier to defend.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Product and Content Teams</h3>
      <p>If you are a product or content team that produces specs, roadmaps, and editorial calendars, Notion is the better fit. The block-based editor is ideal for rich product specs with inline databases, and the multi-view databases let you track tasks, content, and feedback in one place. Notion AI's writing assistance is particularly useful for drafting and summarizing content. For non-engineering teams that want flexibility, Notion wins.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Cross-Functional Organizations</h3>
      <p>If you have a mix of engineering, product, and operations teams, you may need both tools. Many organizations run Notion for product docs, marketing wikis, and company handbooks, while running Confluence for engineering knowledge bases and technical specs tied to Jira. This hybrid approach gives each team the best tool for its workflow, though it requires clear boundaries to avoid duplicate content.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Real-World Use Cases</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Building a startup knowledge base from scratch</h3>
      <p>A 15-person startup needs a wiki for company values, onboarding docs, product specs, and meeting notes. Notion's flat-rate Plus plan at $10 per month covers the entire team. The founder creates a workspace with databases for onboarding tasks, product roadmap, and meeting notes, all in one place. New hires find everything they need in a single tool. Confluence would cost $86.25 per month for the same team and would not offer the same flexibility for non-engineering content.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Documenting an engineering migration</h3>
      <p>A 60-person engineering team is migrating a monolith to microservices and needs to document architecture decisions, RFCs, and runbooks. Confluence's native Jira integration lets engineers embed live Jira issues in spec pages, link tickets to architecture decisions, and track migration progress automatically. The approval workflow ensures a senior engineer signs off on each RFC before it is published. Notion would require third-party Jira integrations and lack the structured governance.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Managing a product roadmap and content calendar</h3>
      <p>A product team needs to track a roadmap, a content calendar, and customer feedback in one place. Notion's databases let the team create a roadmap board view, a content calendar view, and a feedback table view, all from the same underlying data. Notion AI summarizes customer feedback and extracts action items automatically. The team pays $10 per month for the entire workspace. Confluence would require separate tools for content calendar and feedback tracking.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Running a compliance audit for a regulated industry</h3>
      <p>A financial services company needs to document policies, procedures, and controls for a SOC 2 audit. Confluence's granular permissions restrict access to sensitive policies, audit logs track every change, and approval workflows ensure compliance officers sign off on each policy update. The audit team can export a complete history of who changed what and when. Notion's Enterprise plan offers audit logs but lacks the depth of Confluence's governance features for regulated use cases.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Onboarding new engineers with living documentation</h3>
      <p>A 200-person engineering team uses Confluence to maintain living documentation tied to Jira epics. When a new engineer joins, they read the architecture overview, which embeds live Jira filters showing active epics and their status. Each epic links to relevant Confluence specs, and each spec links back to the Jira tickets it describes. The bidirectional linking means documentation never goes stale because it updates automatically as Jira tickets move through workflows. Notion cannot replicate this level of engineering integration.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Final Verdict</h2>
    <div class="verdict">
      <p><strong>Best overall for small to mid-size teams: Notion.</strong> Notion's flat-rate pricing, flexible block editor, and powerful databases make it the best all-in-one workspace for teams under 100 people. At $10 per month for the entire workspace, it is the most cost-effective documentation tool on the market. For startups, product teams, and cross-functional organizations that want one tool for everything, Notion wins.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for enterprise and engineering: Confluence.</strong> Confluence's native Jira integration, granular permissions, audit logs, and compliance certifications make it the standard for enterprise documentation. For engineering teams already in the Atlassian ecosystem, Confluence is the natural choice. For regulated industries that need approval workflows and audit trails, Confluence is the safer bet.</p>
      <p><strong>Best budget choice for growing teams: Notion Plus.</strong> At $10 per month flat for the entire workspace, Notion Plus is unbeatable for teams that are scaling. Even at 50 users, the cost stays at $10 per month, while Confluence Standard would cost $287.50 per month. For cost-conscious teams that do not need Jira integration, Notion is the clear winner.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended combo:</strong> For organizations with both engineering and non-engineering teams, run Notion for product docs, marketing wikis, and company handbooks, and run Confluence for engineering specs and Jira-linked documentation. This hybrid approach gives each team the best tool for its workflow without forcing a one-size-fits-all choice. Total cost for a 50-person team is roughly $297.50 per month, which is comparable to Confluence alone.</p>
    </div>

