    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is Notion better than Confluence?</h3>
    <p>It depends on your use case. Notion is better for small to mid-size teams that want flexibility, flat-rate pricing, and an all-in-one workspace. Confluence is better for enterprise organizations that need deep Jira integration, granular page-level permissions, audit logs, and compliance certifications. There is no universal winner; the right choice depends on team size, technical stack, and governance requirements.</p>

    <h3>Which is more affordable, Notion or Confluence?</h3>
    <p>Notion is significantly more affordable for growing teams. Notion Plus is a flat $10 per month for the entire workspace, while Confluence charges $5.75 per user per month. At 50 users, Notion Plus costs $10 per month versus Confluence's $287.50 per month. For small teams of 1-3 people, Confluence Free can be cheaper, but Notion scales better as headcount grows.</p>

    <h3>Can Notion replace Confluence?</h3>
    <p>Notion can replace Confluence for small to mid-size teams that do not rely on deep Jira integration or enterprise compliance features. However, organizations that require granular page-level permissions, audit logs, approval workflows, and tight Atlassian ecosystem integration will still need Confluence. Many teams run both in parallel, using Notion for product docs and Confluence for engineering knowledge bases.</p>

    <h3>Does Notion integrate with Jira?</h3>
    <p>Notion does not have native deep Jira integration like Confluence does. You can connect Notion to Jira through third-party tools like Zapier, Make, or embed approaches, but you will not get the bidirectional linking of issues, epics, and releases that Confluence offers out of the box. If Jira integration is a core workflow requirement, Confluence is the better choice.</p>

    <h3>Which is better for developers, Notion or Confluence?</h3>
    <p>Confluence is generally better for developer documentation because of its native Jira integration, page templates for RFCs and architecture decisions, code blocks with syntax highlighting, and approval workflows for technical specs. Notion is better for cross-functional developer teams that want to combine docs, project tracking, and product specs in one workspace.</p>

    <h3>Which has better AI features, Notion or Confluence?</h3>
    <p>Both platforms have invested in AI. Notion AI offers writing assistance, summarization, Q&A across your workspace, and autofill for database properties, making it well-suited for content-heavy workflows. Confluence Intelligence, powered by Atlassian Intelligence, focuses on summarizing pages, generating Jira issues from text, and answering questions across Atlassian products. Notion AI is more polished for writers; Confluence AI is stronger for engineering and ops context.</p>

